1295
00:54:24,891 --> 00:54:26,221
Sounds great, doesn't it?

1296
00:54:28,031 --> 00:54:29,261
There we go.

1297
00:54:33,361 --> 00:54:35,800
(He naturally walks into a building.)

1298
00:54:35,801 --> 00:54:37,801
- Why did he come here? - I wonder.

1299
00:54:38,071 --> 00:54:39,471
- To take a class? - As an old student?

1300
00:54:39,671 --> 00:54:40,801
Is he an old student?

1301
00:54:44,311 --> 00:54:45,481
- What? - What is this place?

1302
00:54:46,111 --> 00:54:47,651
Is it a room for meetings?

1303
00:54:48,081 --> 00:54:49,111
He has become a student again?

1304
00:54:49,481 --> 00:54:50,551
Could that be it?

1305
00:54:50,851 --> 00:54:51,921
What's going on?

1306
00:54:54,151 --> 00:54:56,650
Hansei University is a school...

1307
00:54:56,651 --> 00:54:57,920
located in Gunpo, Gyeonggi Province.

1308
00:54:57,921 --> 00:55:00,760
I'm a performing arts professor,

1309
00:55:00,761 --> 00:55:03,290
so I am Professor Jung Ki Yeol.

1310
00:55:03,291 --> 00:55:05,501
- That's another name of mine. - Your name is Jung Ki Yeol?

1311
00:55:06,231 --> 00:55:07,670
- Your name was Ki Yeol? - Yes.

1312
00:55:07,671 --> 00:55:08,900
I didn't know that.

1313
00:55:08,901 --> 00:55:10,530
I thought his last name was Ka.

1314
00:55:10,531 --> 00:55:12,601
I thought that as well.

1315
00:55:12,701 --> 00:55:14,240
You were a full-time professor?

1316
00:55:14,241 --> 00:55:15,270
His name was Ki Yeol.

1317
00:55:15,271 --> 00:55:16,470
He was a professor too.

1318
00:55:16,471 --> 00:55:18,140
- He must be really busy. - If he's a full-time professor...

1319
00:55:18,141 --> 00:55:21,280
Yes. It has been two years...

1320
00:55:21,281 --> 00:55:22,881
since I have been a full-time professor.

1321
00:55:23,181 --> 00:55:26,450
I was a visiting professor for around three years before that.

1322
00:55:26,451 --> 00:55:28,450
- So five years in total. - Yes.

1323
00:55:28,451 --> 00:55:31,860
- I majored in vocal music. - Right.

1324
00:55:31,861 --> 00:55:33,620
When I was a student,

1325
00:55:33,621 --> 00:55:36,390
I didn't expect musicals to become this popular in Korea.

1326
00:55:36,391 --> 00:55:37,431
- I see. - Right.

1327
00:55:37,961 --> 00:55:39,660
There was no one around me...

1328
00:55:39,661 --> 00:55:42,330
to teach me about what to study and work on...

1329
00:55:42,331 --> 00:55:44,130
to become a musical actor.

1330
00:55:44,131 --> 00:55:45,140
Right.

1331
00:55:45,141 --> 00:55:49,711
So as I studied to become a musical actor,

1332
00:55:50,011 --> 00:55:52,340
I wanted to become someone who could teach students...

1333
00:55:52,341 --> 00:55:53,811
with the same struggles that I went through.

1334
00:55:54,181 --> 00:55:56,280
I always had that in mind,

1335
00:55:56,281 --> 00:55:58,521
and I was given a good opportunity...

1336
00:55:58,781 --> 00:56:01,321
to teach at my school.

1337
00:56:01,591 --> 00:56:03,450
Doesn't it take a lot of your time to be a full-time professor?

1338
00:56:03,451 --> 00:56:04,490
Right.

1339
00:56:04,491 --> 00:56:05,891
The professors of the same department...

1340
00:56:06,021 --> 00:56:07,790
know that I have a lot of performances.

1341
00:56:07,791 --> 00:56:09,991
They help me out a lot...

1342
00:56:10,391 --> 00:56:13,930
- and cover for me often as well. - They help you out.

1343
00:56:13,931 --> 00:56:16,171
They really must have wanted you to be a professor at their school.

1344
00:56:16,731 --> 00:56:19,501
It wasn't set up based on a specific interior design.

1345
00:56:19,671 --> 00:56:23,571
I set the place up like that as I didn't want the school...

1346
00:56:23,741 --> 00:56:24,981
to feel soulless.

1347
00:56:25,281 --> 00:56:27,310
- This is Kai's Room. - It's really big.

1348
00:56:27,311 --> 00:56:29,480
- Did you decorate it on your own? - Yes.

1349
00:56:29,481 --> 00:56:30,581
Really?

1350
00:56:31,751 --> 00:56:33,620
It has such a nice atmosphere. Check that out.

1351
00:56:33,621 --> 00:56:34,791
There's a piano as well.

1352
00:56:35,421 --> 00:56:36,591
It's not a regular professor's office.

1353
00:56:37,251 --> 00:56:38,990
I've never seen an office set up like that.

1354
00:56:38,991 --> 00:56:40,660
I thought it was a meeting room.

1355
00:56:40,661 --> 00:56:45,131
(Kai's traces are left around the room.)

1356
00:56:45,931 --> 00:56:47,360
- It's... - What is that?

1357
00:56:47,361 --> 00:56:48,700
- Gosh! - That's intense.

1358
00:56:48,701 --> 00:56:51,240
It's a photoshoot for a magazine that I feel proud of.

1359
00:56:51,241 --> 00:56:53,900
I placed it on one side as a piece of my memory.

1360
00:56:53,901 --> 00:56:55,771
It wasn't out of self-attachment.

1361
00:56:56,041 --> 00:56:58,381
Am I not allowed to love myself a bit?

1362
00:56:58,611 --> 00:57:00,411
- We didn't say anything! - Right.

1363
00:57:00,611 --> 00:57:01,881
When was that?

1364
00:57:02,151 --> 00:57:03,280
How is there a professor like that?

1365
00:57:03,281 --> 00:57:04,980
I performed for "Ben-Hur..."

1366
00:57:04,981 --> 00:57:06,780
- around that time. - "Ben-Hur."

1367
00:57:06,781 --> 00:57:09,420
I started working out for the first time back then.

1368
00:57:09,421 --> 00:57:12,120
People who are in movies or dramas are done...

1369
00:57:12,121 --> 00:57:14,960
after filming for that specific scene.

1370
00:57:14,961 --> 00:57:17,060
- Right. - But musical actors...

1371
00:57:17,061 --> 00:57:19,400
- They have to stay fit. - have to go through...

1372
00:57:19,401 --> 00:57:21,571
two months of practice and three months of performances.

1373
00:57:21,971 --> 00:57:23,930
Carbohydrate levels must be controlled...

1374
00:57:23,931 --> 00:57:25,301
for at least half a year.

1375
00:57:26,271 --> 00:57:27,600
It's hard to maintain a fit body.

1376
00:57:27,601 --> 00:57:29,010
- It's maintained for a while. - Right.

1377
00:57:29,011 --> 00:57:30,410
It has to be maintained for that whole period.

1378
00:57:30,411 --> 00:57:31,641
- That must be really tough. - Right.

1379
00:57:32,641 --> 00:57:35,411
(He naturally turns on the power of different items.)

1380
00:57:36,911 --> 00:57:39,681
(He even sets up the piano.)

1381
00:57:41,621 --> 00:57:43,820
(He reaches out to get everything prepared.)

1382
00:57:43,821 --> 00:57:45,920
It doesn't feel like a professor's office.

1383
00:57:45,921 --> 00:57:47,021
It feels like a bar.

1384
00:57:48,191 --> 00:57:50,631
(His work preparations are over.)

1385
00:57:53,961 --> 00:57:55,200
He looks impressive...

1386
00:57:55,201 --> 00:57:56,631
- He looks great. - as a professor.

1387
00:57:58,171 --> 00:58:00,171
(He heads somewhere right after getting to work.)

1388
00:58:01,541 --> 00:58:03,511
(He walks while putting on a professor's smile.)

1389
00:58:04,211 --> 00:58:06,011
- Hello. - Hello.

1390
00:58:06,311 --> 00:58:07,311
Hello.

1391
00:58:17,151 --> 00:58:18,290
Then...

1392
00:58:18,291 --> 00:58:19,821
(The school cafeteria that has meals ranging from 3 to 6 dollars.)

1393
00:58:20,061 --> 00:58:21,120
I'll go with pork cutlet.

1394
00:58:21,121 --> 00:58:22,791
(He chooses the pork cutlet right away.)

1395
00:58:23,261 --> 00:58:26,160
I love having meals in trays.

1396
00:58:26,161 --> 00:58:27,200
Same here.

1397
00:58:27,201 --> 00:58:28,900
- I have those trays at home. - They bring out a special vibe.

1398
00:58:28,901 --> 00:58:29,931
- Right. - Right.

1399
00:58:30,071 --> 00:58:32,741
The food that gets served at school tastes really good.

1400
00:58:32,971 --> 00:58:35,840
But I have a small complaint.

1401
00:58:35,841 --> 00:58:37,410
The food for the teaching staff...

1402
00:58:37,411 --> 00:58:38,980
keeps getting served based on the taste of elders.

1403
00:58:38,981 --> 00:58:40,011
Seaweed soup.

1404
00:58:40,181 --> 00:58:41,181
That looks good.

1405
00:58:41,182 --> 00:58:42,480
The food for the teaching staff suits my taste.

1406
00:58:42,481 --> 00:58:43,481
But for the students...

1407
00:58:43,482 --> 00:58:44,620
The students are served tastier dishes.

1408
00:58:44,621 --> 00:58:45,750
- Pork cutlet. - Pork cutlet.

1409
00:58:45,751 --> 00:58:47,120
Dishes like udon get served often.

1410
00:58:47,121 --> 00:58:49,190
Also... Am I allowed to say this?

1411
00:58:49,191 --> 00:58:50,650
When I go to the cafeteria for the teaching staff,

1412
00:58:50,651 --> 00:58:54,260
there are so many older professors who are eating.

1413
00:58:54,261 --> 00:58:55,330
So I have to greet all of them.

1414
00:58:55,331 --> 00:58:56,830
- "Professor Jung Ki Yeol!" - "Oh, hello, sir."

1415
00:58:56,831 --> 00:58:57,960
- "Professor Jung!" - "Hello."

1416
00:58:57,961 --> 00:58:59,160
- "I enjoyed your performance." - "Gosh, thank you."

1417
00:58:59,161 --> 00:59:00,161
"Are you performing these days?"

1418
00:59:00,162 --> 00:59:01,300
"Professor Jung!"

1419
00:59:01,301 --> 00:59:02,700
But when you go to the students' cafeteria,

1420
00:59:02,701 --> 00:59:04,170
the students will think that way about you.

1421
00:59:04,171 --> 00:59:05,341
- They will think that way. - Right.

1422
00:59:05,401 --> 00:59:06,540
"Why does that professor..."

1423
00:59:06,541 --> 00:59:07,770
- That was a great point. - "keep coming here?"

1424
00:59:07,771 --> 00:59:08,840
- "It's hard to eat." - You'll be called tactless.

1425
00:59:08,841 --> 00:59:11,140
- "What is that professor doing?" - I just realised that.

1426
00:59:11,141 --> 00:59:12,740
- Right. - The students might think that.

1427
00:59:12,741 --> 00:59:14,411
I see.

1428
00:59:14,751 --> 00:59:17,410
Pork cutlet is one of my favourite foods.

1429
00:59:17,411 --> 00:59:19,850
That was why I chose to have pork cutlet.

1430
00:59:19,851 --> 00:59:22,251
Many people have the wrong idea about me.

1431
00:59:23,591 --> 00:59:25,860
- "He's so strict with his food." - Right.

1432
00:59:25,861 --> 00:59:27,020
People think I only eat things that don't taste good.

1433
00:59:27,021 --> 00:59:28,090
That's terrible.

1434
00:59:28,091 --> 00:59:31,401
I just have a light breakfast.

1435
00:59:31,531 --> 00:59:33,101
I love things like gukbap.

1436
00:59:33,201 --> 00:59:36,071
I love naengmyeon as well.

1437
00:59:36,801 --> 00:59:38,370
You didn't have a body of a gukbap lover.

1438
00:59:38,371 --> 00:59:39,971
I love sundae gukbap.

1439
00:59:41,571 --> 00:59:43,010
(He walks with light steps at the thought of having pork cutlet.)

1440
00:59:43,011 --> 00:59:44,071
Hello.

1441
00:59:46,681 --> 00:59:47,681
(Peeking)

1442
00:59:47,682 --> 00:59:49,210
The students are pretending they haven't seen him.

1443
00:59:49,211 --> 00:59:50,450
- They must feel uncomfortable. - Right.

1444
00:59:50,451 --> 00:59:52,351
So I was making them feel uncomfortable.

1445
00:59:52,551 --> 00:59:53,621
Gosh!

1446
00:59:54,051 --> 00:59:55,850
(Don't avoid me.)

1447
00:59:55,851 --> 00:59:57,591
(Impressed)

1448
00:59:59,491 --> 01:00:02,191
(He gets a generous amount of rice.)

1449
01:00:03,761 --> 01:00:04,831
Thank you.

1450
01:00:05,301 --> 01:00:06,301
Thank you.

1451
01:00:06,901 --> 01:00:08,570
Please give me a lot of soup, ma'am.

1452
01:00:08,571 --> 01:00:10,101
(Smiling)

1453
01:00:11,571 --> 01:00:13,541
- Would you like more lettuce? - Yes, please!

1454
01:00:14,401 --> 01:00:15,571
Please give me a lot.

1455
01:00:16,211 --> 01:00:17,240
Thank you.

1456
01:00:17,241 --> 01:00:18,380
(Filling up the plate)

1457
01:00:18,381 --> 01:00:20,741
- She gave him so much. - That's quite a lot.

1458
01:00:21,141 --> 01:00:22,251
Thank you.

1459
01:00:24,311 --> 01:00:26,581
- Enjoy your meal. - Thank you.

1460
01:00:28,591 --> 01:00:30,651
He speaks in a pleasant manner.

1461
01:00:33,421 --> 01:00:34,461
Hi.

1462
01:00:35,861 --> 01:00:37,160
"Let's get up quickly."

1463
01:00:37,161 --> 01:00:38,161
(Joking)

1464
01:00:38,601 --> 01:00:39,660
They would mouth the words.

1465
01:00:39,661 --> 01:00:41,060
I had no idea they would feel that way.

1466
01:00:41,061 --> 01:00:42,631
- "Smile." - "Gosh, it feels so uncomfortable."

1467
01:00:42,871 --> 01:00:44,030
"Smile."

1468
01:00:44,031 --> 01:00:45,971
(Slicing)

1469
01:00:46,771 --> 01:00:48,841
Gosh, that looks good. It's making me want some.

1470
01:00:51,041 --> 01:00:52,881
Gosh, it's crispy.

1471
01:00:54,911 --> 01:00:56,281
That's a great privilege.

1472
01:00:56,651 --> 01:00:58,481
- Right. Along with that soup. - It's half-transparent.

1473
01:00:59,881 --> 01:01:01,121
Gosh, that looks good!

1474
01:01:04,251 --> 01:01:07,391
(Taking a big bite)

1475
01:01:07,961 --> 01:01:10,191
(This is indeed my favourite food.)

1476
01:01:10,761 --> 01:01:12,261
I can't help it. I'm having pork cutlet later.

1477
01:01:12,761 --> 01:01:14,500
It feels very nice to see university students.

1478
01:01:14,501 --> 01:01:16,400
Right. There's a young energy that can be felt from them.

1479
01:01:16,401 --> 01:01:18,070
There's a certain energy that can be felt from school cafeterias.

1480
01:01:18,071 --> 01:01:19,600
- Right. - After eating at the cafeteria,

1481
01:01:19,601 --> 01:01:21,041
it's the best feeling to skip class.

1482
01:01:21,371 --> 01:01:22,540
It's the best feeling...

1483
01:01:22,541 --> 01:01:23,870
- to skip class. - Right.

1484
01:01:23,871 --> 01:01:25,240
Having some beer between classes...

1485
01:01:25,241 --> 01:01:26,710
- and deciding to skip class. - Gosh.

1486
01:01:26,711 --> 01:01:27,880
That's the best feeling.

1487
01:01:27,881 --> 01:01:29,111
You get put on academic probation right after that.

1488
01:01:31,181 --> 01:01:32,651
You then beg the professor for forgiveness in tears via email.

1489
01:01:32,981 --> 01:01:35,250
- The email of tears. - The email of tears.

1490
01:01:35,251 --> 01:01:36,351
"Professor!"

1491
01:01:37,051 --> 01:01:38,360
- When the weather is nice, - "Just this once!"

1492
01:01:38,361 --> 01:01:39,821
you really don't want to be in class.

1493
01:01:40,391 --> 01:01:43,661
(He also takes a bite of the fresh salad.)

1494
01:01:46,061 --> 01:01:47,161
That must be good.

1495
01:01:50,731 --> 01:01:54,141
(He takes a sip of the soft cream soup as well.)

1496
01:01:59,141 --> 01:02:00,940
(This is it.)

1497
01:02:00,941 --> 01:02:02,181
It's the Solitary Gourmet.

1498
01:02:03,481 --> 01:02:06,121
(Even when he ate sushi in Jeju...)

1499
01:02:08,721 --> 01:02:11,520
It seems like he really loves food.

1500
01:02:11,521 --> 01:02:12,720
He enjoys his food.

1501
01:02:12,721 --> 01:02:14,561
Right. He savours the taste.

1502
01:02:17,291 --> 01:02:19,661
(This is the cafeteria pork cutlet.)

1503
01:02:20,001 --> 01:02:21,161
(Sighing)

1504
01:02:24,071 --> 01:02:25,541
He's eating cafeteria pork cutlet...

1505
01:02:25,771 --> 01:02:27,671
and making it seem like a photoshoot!

1506
01:02:28,941 --> 01:02:30,210
This is what the students will say behind him.

1507
01:02:30,211 --> 01:02:31,810
"There he goes again."

1508
01:02:31,811 --> 01:02:32,981
"Why is he sitting there?"

1509
01:02:33,281 --> 01:02:34,611
(Laughing)

1510
01:02:35,581 --> 01:02:36,810
I want cafeteria food.

1511
01:02:36,811 --> 01:02:38,321
I really want cafeteria food too.

1512
01:02:38,881 --> 01:02:40,821
- It's of good quality. - The food is great.

1513
01:02:41,251 --> 01:02:43,091
You never feel full with cafeteria food.

1514
01:02:45,121 --> 01:02:47,121
(Smiling)

1515
01:02:51,261 --> 01:02:52,931
(Can you feel it?)

1516
01:02:54,301 --> 01:02:57,801
(Can you feel this fantastic taste?)

1517
01:03:00,171 --> 01:03:01,710
(The fireworks of flavour mesmerize his tongue.)

1518
01:03:01,711 --> 01:03:02,940
Is this the right song to be playing at this moment?

1519
01:03:02,941 --> 01:03:05,281
Is there a need for this while eating cafeteria food?

1520
01:03:05,811 --> 01:03:07,981
(It's good, isn't it?)

1521
01:03:09,181 --> 01:03:10,581
Who is he doing that to?

1522
01:03:11,011 --> 01:03:12,951
Isn't he just doing that on his own?

1523
01:03:13,451 --> 01:03:15,391
It's creepy. There's no one there!

1524
01:03:17,051 --> 01:03:18,221
What's going on?

1525
01:03:18,461 --> 01:03:19,991
People who don't have friends do that.

1526
01:03:20,261 --> 01:03:22,360
They would act like they're greeting others.

1527
01:03:22,361 --> 01:03:23,760
They would put their jackets on the other side.

1528
01:03:23,761 --> 01:03:25,300
And the students sitting at a different table would go,

1529
01:03:25,301 --> 01:03:26,500
"There he goes again."

1530
01:03:26,501 --> 01:03:28,670
- "Look over there." - "There he goes again."

1531
01:03:28,671 --> 01:03:29,871
"There he goes again."

1532
01:03:34,201 --> 01:03:38,511
(He finishes his meal in a flash.)

1533
01:03:39,911 --> 01:03:41,981
(The last bite)

1534
01:03:43,451 --> 01:03:45,281
(He pleasantly finishes his 5-dollar meal.)

1535
01:03:47,351 --> 01:03:49,191
Isn't that great for five dollars?

1536
01:03:49,551 --> 01:03:50,820
- That's a great meal. - Of course.

1537
01:03:50,821 --> 01:03:51,890
- Definitely. - If you consider...

1538
01:03:51,891 --> 01:03:52,921
the prices these days...

1539
01:03:53,021 --> 01:03:55,060
A few pieces of Pikachu pork cutlet would cost five dollars.

1540
01:03:55,061 --> 01:03:56,760
No. One piece costs three dollars.

1541
01:03:56,761 --> 01:03:57,830
- They're expensive these days. - What?

1542
01:03:57,831 --> 01:03:59,731
- Three dollars for a small piece. - You Pikachu as well?

1543
01:04:01,201 --> 01:04:03,231
(He cleans up neatly as well.)

1544
01:04:04,271 --> 01:04:07,201
(I should get back soon.)

1545
01:04:08,671 --> 01:04:12,441
(Professor Kai heads to the cafe after eating.)

1546
01:04:16,781 --> 01:04:17,881
Here you are.

1547
01:04:18,081 --> 01:04:20,551
(Rummaging)

1548
01:04:21,221 --> 01:04:22,320
- Here it is. - Is it a coupon?

1549
01:04:22,321 --> 01:04:23,850
These...

1550
01:04:23,851 --> 01:04:25,720
are my students' cards.

1551
01:04:25,721 --> 01:04:26,821
- What is going on? - Right.

1552
01:04:27,361 --> 01:04:29,361
Are you getting their stamp cards stamped for them?

1553
01:04:29,861 --> 01:04:30,861
You get it stamped for them?

1554
01:04:30,862 --> 01:04:32,361
Each time he buys coffee.

1555
01:04:32,801 --> 01:04:33,831
That's sweet of you.

1556
01:04:34,031 --> 01:04:36,771
Have you ever thought about using one of their stamp cards?

1557
01:04:37,201 --> 01:04:38,441
(No way!)

1558
01:04:39,071 --> 01:04:40,300
Thank you.

1559
01:04:40,301 --> 01:04:42,070
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1560
01:04:42,071 --> 01:04:43,670
The stamp cards are left there?

1561
01:04:43,671 --> 01:04:47,410
Yes. Their names are written there.

1562
01:04:47,411 --> 01:04:48,551
They get their own stamp cards stamped.

1563
01:04:48,651 --> 01:04:50,481
(After getting the stamps...)

1564
01:04:51,651 --> 01:04:54,081
Right. He got four cups of coffee.

1565
01:04:55,091 --> 01:04:56,821
(Greeting pleasantly)

1566
01:04:58,921 --> 01:05:00,221
Hello, Professor.

1567
01:05:01,061 --> 01:05:02,331
It's a different professor.

1568
01:05:03,931 --> 01:05:05,461
(Walking quickly)

1569
01:05:06,161 --> 01:05:07,701
He walks quickly.

1570
01:05:10,071 --> 01:05:11,301
- I'll see you later. - Gosh.

1571
01:05:11,471 --> 01:05:12,741
He wants good grades from him.

1572
01:05:13,241 --> 01:05:15,171
There's nothing he's positive about.

1573
01:05:17,141 --> 01:05:18,380
He's getting meaner and meaner.

1574
01:05:18,381 --> 01:05:19,381
(Joking)

1575
01:05:20,711 --> 01:05:23,280
(Has he returned to Kai's Room?)

1576
01:05:23,281 --> 01:05:24,581
Hello.

1577
01:05:24,811 --> 01:05:26,621
- Who are they? - According to your tastes.

1578
01:05:27,321 --> 01:05:28,350
Thank you.

1579
01:05:28,351 --> 01:05:29,690
- This one is sweet. - Because they're your seniors?

1580
01:05:29,691 --> 01:05:31,550
Yes. It was the day of a professor's meeting.

1581
01:05:31,551 --> 01:05:35,261
There are four full-time professors.

1582
01:05:35,391 --> 01:05:39,461
We have a full-time faculty meeting once or twice a week.

1583
01:05:39,531 --> 01:05:40,831
We have a group chat too.

1584
01:05:41,001 --> 01:05:44,170
There isn't a specific meeting room, so we normally gather...

1585
01:05:44,171 --> 01:05:47,170
in the department head's room. That's what we normally do,

1586
01:05:47,171 --> 01:05:50,871
but my room became like a gathering lounge.

1587
01:05:51,211 --> 01:05:53,341
- There are many times... - Because it's well-decorated.

1588
01:05:53,641 --> 01:05:55,281
where the meeting is held in my room naturally.

1589
01:05:55,511 --> 01:05:58,921
They're a lot older than me,

1590
01:05:59,081 --> 01:06:00,721
and they're more experienced.

1591
01:06:01,151 --> 01:06:02,821
I'm the absolute youngest.

1592
01:06:03,221 --> 01:06:06,161
I'm so loved by the professors.

1593
01:06:06,361 --> 01:06:07,921
They don't ask me to get anything,

1594
01:06:08,021 --> 01:06:10,861
- but I do to be on their good side. - He's Mr. Suck-Up.

1595
01:06:11,261 --> 01:06:12,830
- Yes, Mr. Suck-Up. - You ought to be one.

1596
01:06:12,831 --> 01:06:14,861
- He sure is Mr. Suck-Up. - Mr. Suck-Up?

1597
01:06:15,131 --> 01:06:17,571
I get drinks according to their tastes.

1598
01:06:17,971 --> 01:06:20,470
A hot Americano for the department head,

1599
01:06:20,471 --> 01:06:22,811
a vanilla latte for Professor Oh,

1600
01:06:22,971 --> 01:06:26,981
yuzu ade for Professor Lee Dong Geun,

1601
01:06:27,111 --> 01:06:30,311
- and an iced Americano for me. - Iced Americano?

1602
01:06:30,551 --> 01:06:33,721
This clip itself is a video letter...

1603
01:06:33,951 --> 01:06:35,391
- for Hue Park. - Tell me about it.

1604
01:06:36,351 --> 01:06:37,791
- It's a self-introduction video. - It's a profile.

1605
01:06:38,021 --> 01:06:39,391
"Take a look at my video profile."

1606
01:06:39,591 --> 01:06:42,091
His everyday life is filled with flattery.

1607
01:06:42,361 --> 01:06:44,661
It's not that there's a target for his flattery today.

1608
01:06:44,891 --> 01:06:45,960
We're seeing the everyday life...

1609
01:06:45,961 --> 01:06:47,800
- of a flatterer. - Exactly. The life of a flatterer.

1610
01:06:47,801 --> 01:06:49,130
- The life of a flatterer. - The life of a flatterer.

1611
01:06:49,131 --> 01:06:51,401
- How to survive with flattery. - Jabbering.

1612
01:06:52,041 --> 01:06:53,900
I've never seen someone with an image of a flatterer.

1613
01:06:53,901 --> 01:06:55,540
- It's cute. - It's really cute.

1614
01:06:55,541 --> 01:06:57,811
- I like it. - It'll go crazy on Christmas.

1615
01:06:58,171 --> 01:06:59,311
Jung Flatterer.

1616
01:07:00,011 --> 01:07:01,810
Let's quickly go through the full-time faculty meeting...

1617
01:07:01,811 --> 01:07:04,150
- since everyone has a class. - All right.

1618
01:07:04,151 --> 01:07:06,251
We're preparing an event for the 20th anniversary...

1619
01:07:06,681 --> 01:07:09,221
- of our department. - That's a big event.

1620
01:07:09,791 --> 01:07:10,850
Professor Kai,

1621
01:07:10,851 --> 01:07:12,361
- give us your opinion. - All right.

1622
01:07:13,221 --> 01:07:16,790
How about you host the event, Professor Kai?

1623
01:07:16,791 --> 01:07:18,760
- Please host the event. - All right.

1624
01:07:18,761 --> 01:07:20,000
- Okay. - Also, how about...

1625
01:07:20,001 --> 01:07:22,071
- a narration? - Sounds good.

1626
01:07:22,331 --> 01:07:24,601
- Please check on it. Music too. - All right.

1627
01:07:25,271 --> 01:07:27,801
I thought it'd be nice to have such elements.

1628
01:07:27,941 --> 01:07:30,370
We need to decide on the title of the show.

1629
01:07:30,371 --> 01:07:33,110
Let's focus on the future instead of the past.

1630
01:07:33,111 --> 01:07:35,880
He looks like a little kid among the professors.

1631
01:07:35,881 --> 01:07:37,311
- He looks like a new recruit. - Yes.

1632
01:07:38,181 --> 01:07:39,681
- He also feels like Eui Chan. - Yes.

1633
01:07:40,221 --> 01:07:41,520
He looks like a smart boy.

1634
01:07:41,521 --> 01:07:43,021
(Professor Kai looks smart for some reason.)

1635
01:07:44,151 --> 01:07:45,360
What do you think, Professor Kai?

1636
01:07:45,361 --> 01:07:46,660
- Yes. - That's scary.

1637
01:07:46,661 --> 01:07:48,491
- What do you think, Professor Kai? - What do you think, Professor Jung?

1638
01:07:49,361 --> 01:07:50,461
I think it sounds good.

1639
01:07:50,861 --> 01:07:52,501
- I think it sounds good. - He thinks everything is good.

1640
01:07:53,201 --> 01:07:54,461
(He finds him cute.)

1641
01:07:54,761 --> 01:07:56,731
They don't look like scary seniors.

1642
01:07:58,171 --> 01:08:01,811
Or Professor Kai could say something in the middle.

1643
01:08:02,511 --> 01:08:03,840
How about you being in charge...

1644
01:08:03,841 --> 01:08:05,480
- of comments? - Okay, I got it.

1645
01:08:05,481 --> 01:08:07,941
A narration or any comments for promotion.

1646
01:08:09,351 --> 01:08:10,480
(Onstage, he's full of charisma.)

1647
01:08:10,481 --> 01:08:12,220
Yes, he's really amazing.

1648
01:08:12,221 --> 01:08:13,580
He's the king of the musical scene.

1649
01:08:13,581 --> 01:08:14,621
Of course.

1650
01:08:16,121 --> 01:08:18,590
I thought it'd be nice to have such elements.

1651
01:08:18,591 --> 01:08:20,991
I think it'd be nice for the students if you dropped by.

1652
01:08:21,191 --> 01:08:23,730
- Please be in charge. - All right.

1653
01:08:23,731 --> 01:08:25,531
- For the graduate school... - Yes.

1654
01:08:26,431 --> 01:08:29,030
We'll leave most of it to Professor Kai.

1655
01:08:29,031 --> 01:08:30,730
- That's... - Because he keeps saying yes to it.

1656
01:08:30,731 --> 01:08:32,141
- All right. - They're making you do everything.

1657
01:08:32,871 --> 01:08:34,040
- All right. - Okay.

1658
01:08:34,041 --> 01:08:36,011
Do you ever say no?

1659
01:08:36,311 --> 01:08:37,870
- It'll be too hard. - "I don't think I can do it."

1660
01:08:37,871 --> 01:08:38,880
Well, they give me tasks...

1661
01:08:38,881 --> 01:08:41,440
- that I can manage, to begin with. - Goodness.

1662
01:08:41,441 --> 01:08:43,150
- That's a great mindset. - Yes.

1663
01:08:43,151 --> 01:08:45,420
You tend to take good care of a junior like him.

1664
01:08:45,421 --> 01:08:46,520
- Of course. - You'd take good care.

1665
01:08:46,521 --> 01:08:47,551
That must be why he's loved.

1666
01:08:47,781 --> 01:08:50,751
You've done many concerts,

1667
01:08:51,051 --> 01:08:53,061
so I think you're someone...

1668
01:08:53,421 --> 01:08:55,730
who can see the overall picture best among us.

1669
01:08:55,731 --> 01:08:59,160
Shall I get congratulatory videos from actors?

1670
01:08:59,161 --> 01:09:01,661
- He's making unnecessary work. - That'd be great.

1671
01:09:02,371 --> 01:09:03,631
He's the type to make more work.

1672
01:09:04,131 --> 01:09:05,370
- Can we include that? - That'd be lovely.

1673
01:09:05,371 --> 01:09:06,601
- All right. - That'd be great.

1674
01:09:07,471 --> 01:09:09,371
If you take a look at it,

1675
01:09:09,541 --> 01:09:12,041
- you'll have a clearer picture. - Yes.

1676
01:09:12,211 --> 01:09:14,080
It looks like Professor Lee...

1677
01:09:14,081 --> 01:09:18,581
- took detailed care of it. - My goodness.

1678
01:09:19,751 --> 01:09:21,251
- Thank you. - Sure.

1679
01:09:21,581 --> 01:09:24,051
That was the most touching comment.

1680
01:09:24,591 --> 01:09:26,561
- We're wrapping up amicably. - Yes.

1681
01:09:26,891 --> 01:09:29,360
- Let's finish up the meeting. - All right.

1682
01:09:29,361 --> 01:09:31,900
- Great work, everyone. - Thank you.

1683
01:09:31,901 --> 01:09:33,530
- Great work. - All right.

1684
01:09:33,531 --> 01:09:34,531
(I did well today, as usual.)

1685
01:09:34,661 --> 01:09:36,430
I'm off for lunch.

1686
01:09:36,431 --> 01:09:39,571
(Bowing)

1687
01:09:39,801 --> 01:09:41,670
- I'll get going. - Okay.

1688
01:09:41,671 --> 01:09:43,441
(He keeps bowing.)

1689
01:09:43,971 --> 01:09:45,280
- Please take good care of it. - He's doing well socially.

1690
01:09:45,281 --> 01:09:46,440
- All right. - Thank you.

1691
01:09:46,441 --> 01:09:48,311
- I should learn from him. - It's difficult.

1692
01:09:48,341 --> 01:09:50,651
He's watching them from the back too.

1693
01:09:51,951 --> 01:09:53,951
He's bowing to their shadows too.

1694
01:09:54,281 --> 01:09:56,391
(Polite Mr. Suck-Up returns to his room.)

1695
01:09:57,521 --> 01:09:59,720
(Taking out his laptop)

1696
01:09:59,721 --> 01:10:01,160
(To be continued)

1697
01:10:01,161 --> 01:10:04,561
(He's busy even after the meeting.)

1698
01:10:04,961 --> 01:10:06,030
(Squeaking)

1699
01:10:06,031 --> 01:10:07,501
Hello, Professor.

1700
01:10:08,501 --> 01:10:09,671
Oh, Seung Yoon, come on in.

1701
01:10:10,946 --> 01:10:12,446
Hello, Professor.

1702
01:10:12,916 --> 01:10:14,616
- Who is it? - Is he a TA?

1703
01:10:15,286 --> 01:10:16,355
Is he a TA?

1704
01:10:16,356 --> 01:10:17,755
(He sits naturally in front of Professor Kai.)

1705
01:10:17,756 --> 01:10:18,885
Come on in.

1706
01:10:18,886 --> 01:10:20,086
Hello.

1707
01:10:20,726 --> 01:10:22,156
- Hello. - Hi. Welcome.

1708
01:10:22,826 --> 01:10:24,255
- I'm sorry, Professor. - Hi, come on in.

1709
01:10:24,256 --> 01:10:25,326
- His students? - Hi.

1710
01:10:26,096 --> 01:10:27,595
- Hello, ma'am. - Hello.

1711
01:10:27,596 --> 01:10:28,965
- Hello. - Hello.

1712
01:10:28,966 --> 01:10:30,135
A teacher?

1713
01:10:30,136 --> 01:10:31,235
She's the pianist.

1714
01:10:31,236 --> 01:10:32,405
(The accompanist sits in front of the piano.)

1715
01:10:32,406 --> 01:10:34,836
It's the class I spend the most time on at school.

1716
01:10:35,106 --> 01:10:36,735
It's a major practical class.

1717
01:10:36,736 --> 01:10:40,546
I teach them 1-on-1,

1718
01:10:40,806 --> 01:10:43,115
or I teach them in a group, like an open lecture.

1719
01:10:43,116 --> 01:10:46,045
I teach their singing in depth.

1720
01:10:46,046 --> 01:10:48,156
Today's the day I teach a major lecture.

1721
01:10:48,616 --> 01:10:50,555
- Oh, it's a class. - That's where you have class.

1722
01:10:50,556 --> 01:10:51,955
- Yes. - You have class there?

1723
01:10:51,956 --> 01:10:54,156
- Yes. - Isn't there a practice room?

1724
01:10:54,696 --> 01:10:56,426
There is, but my office...

1725
01:10:56,696 --> 01:10:59,066
- helps them focus better. - That must be more fun.

1726
01:10:59,626 --> 01:11:02,196
The students must be so happy to get a direct lesson from him.

1727
01:11:02,336 --> 01:11:03,965
Is it possible to get a private lesson...

1728
01:11:03,966 --> 01:11:05,606
from someone at the top of the industry?

1729
01:11:05,836 --> 01:11:08,036
- It's a really precious time. - Gosh, Professor Kai...

1730
01:11:09,136 --> 01:11:10,376
Shall we begin with you, Seung Yoon?

1731
01:11:11,206 --> 01:11:12,376
Yes, sir.

1732
01:11:12,906 --> 01:11:16,476
- Why don't you sing here today? - Okay.

1733
01:11:17,116 --> 01:11:18,346
Which year are they in?

1734
01:11:18,486 --> 01:11:20,845
They're seniors who are about to graduate.

1735
01:11:20,846 --> 01:11:22,756
- You'll get to see them onstage. - That's right.

1736
01:11:23,516 --> 01:11:25,325
(Nervous)

1737
01:11:25,326 --> 01:11:27,956
(She starts playing the piano.)

1738
01:11:28,356 --> 01:11:31,096
(Let's give it a listen.)

1739
01:11:31,966 --> 01:11:34,395
("Since I'm Alive" from the musical, "Ben-Hur")

1740
01:11:34,396 --> 01:11:35,596
Gosh, he has a nice voice.

1741
01:11:36,466 --> 01:11:39,006
(As soon as the lesson starts, Mr. Suck-Up disappears.)

1742
01:11:39,766 --> 01:11:44,876
(The look in Professor Kai's eyes has changed.)

1743
01:11:46,046 --> 01:11:50,446
(Soon, the song reaches its climax.)

1744
01:11:51,076 --> 01:11:52,415
(He's become serious for some reason.)

1745
01:11:52,416 --> 01:11:54,216
(Thinking)

1746
01:11:54,786 --> 01:11:57,385
(Even if it's just for once)

1747
01:11:57,386 --> 01:11:58,656
- That's not good. - Okay.

1748
01:11:59,056 --> 01:12:02,096
(Even if it's just for once)

1749
01:12:02,556 --> 01:12:05,696
(He points out the part the student needs to fix.)

1750
01:12:06,526 --> 01:12:09,096
You should keep singing that part in the same tone.

1751
01:12:09,466 --> 01:12:12,465
You suddenly lose breath if you sing like that.

1752
01:12:12,466 --> 01:12:14,676
Then you can't hit the high notes that come after.

1753
01:12:14,936 --> 01:12:17,175
- You only need to fix that. - Okay.

1754
01:12:17,176 --> 01:12:18,275
- Okay? - Yes.

1755
01:12:18,276 --> 01:12:20,275
- Great. Seong Beom, you're next. - Okay.

1756
01:12:20,276 --> 01:12:22,515
(He wraps up with a compliment. The next student sings.)

1757
01:12:22,516 --> 01:12:25,115
("The Hymn of Death")

1758
01:12:25,116 --> 01:12:26,116
I knew it.

1759
01:12:26,746 --> 01:12:28,616
- He has great vocal projection. - It's nice.

1760
01:12:30,356 --> 01:12:33,686
(But Professor Kai seems serious.)

1761
01:12:34,526 --> 01:12:36,396
(The end of life)

1762
01:12:37,896 --> 01:12:39,066
All right.

1763
01:12:40,466 --> 01:12:42,966
You have a great voice.

1764
01:12:43,066 --> 01:12:45,135
- Goodness. - He's like ZO ZAZZ...

1765
01:12:45,136 --> 01:12:46,205
of the musical scene.

1766
01:12:46,206 --> 01:12:48,135
- He has that vibe. - ZO ZAZZ?

1767
01:12:48,136 --> 01:12:49,436
He does have that vibe.

1768
01:12:49,806 --> 01:12:51,906
But...

1769
01:12:52,806 --> 01:12:53,876
But...

1770
01:12:54,716 --> 01:12:56,076
"But?" That's scary.

1771
01:12:56,546 --> 01:12:58,885
I wonder if your voice could go on a diet.

1772
01:12:58,886 --> 01:13:00,046
A diet of his voice?

1773
01:13:00,446 --> 01:13:01,916
You can go on a diet with your voice?

1774
01:13:02,156 --> 01:13:03,955
Are you saying I shouldn't go overboard?

1775
01:13:03,956 --> 01:13:06,355
It's good that your emotional energy is added to it,

1776
01:13:06,356 --> 01:13:08,355
but you can use less of that energy.

1777
01:13:08,356 --> 01:13:09,356
Okay.

1778
01:13:10,156 --> 01:13:11,595
You can go on a diet with your voice?

1779
01:13:11,596 --> 01:13:15,195
If you try to use a full-bodied sound,

1780
01:13:15,196 --> 01:13:16,466
you use extra strength.

1781
01:13:16,636 --> 01:13:18,465
- It becomes too much. - It's really difficult...

1782
01:13:18,466 --> 01:13:20,066
to maintain your voice in a pleasant tone.

1783
01:13:20,306 --> 01:13:22,136
Especially in musicals,

1784
01:13:22,436 --> 01:13:24,635
you sing about 20 songs...

1785
01:13:24,636 --> 01:13:26,676
- for three hours. - That's right.

1786
01:13:26,946 --> 01:13:28,415
So if you fail to control your vocal cords from the start,

1787
01:13:28,416 --> 01:13:29,446
it can become difficult.

1788
01:13:29,916 --> 01:13:31,375
It's not like you perform...

1789
01:13:31,376 --> 01:13:32,715
- for three hours only on that day. - That's not the case.

1790
01:13:32,716 --> 01:13:34,755
- You perform the next day too. - You're right.

1791
01:13:34,756 --> 01:13:36,015
He can give that kind of feedback...

1792
01:13:36,016 --> 01:13:37,056
- because he's still active. - He can give that.

1793
01:13:37,556 --> 01:13:39,625
You must've figured out the pros and cons of each student.

1794
01:13:39,626 --> 01:13:41,156
I totally have them figured out.

1795
01:13:41,356 --> 01:13:44,125
If your voice could go on a diet a little, you can hit...

1796
01:13:44,126 --> 01:13:45,325
- I mean your voice. - Yes.

1797
01:13:45,326 --> 01:13:47,165
- If your voice could go on a diet, - It's a little...

1798
01:13:47,166 --> 01:13:49,435
you can reach a tone 2 or 3 notes higher.

1799
01:13:49,436 --> 01:13:50,666
I see. Okay.

1800
01:13:51,266 --> 01:13:54,365
(Let me show you how it's sung.)

1801
01:13:54,366 --> 01:13:56,936
- It's not this. The waves - Okay.

1802
01:13:57,736 --> 01:14:00,375
- It needs to ripple more lightly. - Oh, a diet like that.

1803
01:14:00,376 --> 01:14:03,276
Don't show your strength like that at once.

1804
01:14:03,876 --> 01:14:04,946
Keep it first...

1805
01:14:05,716 --> 01:14:06,945
(Should I take it out or not?)

1806
01:14:06,946 --> 01:14:08,015
- You get what I mean, right? - Yes.

1807
01:14:08,016 --> 01:14:10,186
You know swordsmen, right? With a sword like this.

1808
01:14:10,656 --> 01:14:13,256
If you take the sword out, you can get rid of them at once,

1809
01:14:14,556 --> 01:14:15,825
but you hold it.

1810
01:14:15,826 --> 01:14:16,955
- Goodness. - Okay.

1811
01:14:16,956 --> 01:14:18,666
What does it mean? I don't get it.

1812
01:14:18,896 --> 01:14:21,195
- So you have to tease. - Exactly.

1813
01:14:21,196 --> 01:14:22,735
- He's indeed good. - Like a see-through.

1814
01:14:22,736 --> 01:14:23,936
He's indeed good.

1815
01:14:24,566 --> 01:14:26,306
"See-through?" What do you mean?

1816
01:14:26,836 --> 01:14:27,906
Don't take off everything.

1817
01:14:28,806 --> 01:14:30,706
It's like an audition show.

1818
01:14:31,546 --> 01:14:32,746
I understood.

1819
01:14:33,076 --> 01:14:34,375
- You can totally do it. - Okay.

1820
01:14:34,376 --> 01:14:35,675
(Professor Kai wraps up with a compliment and encouragement.)

1821
01:14:35,676 --> 01:14:37,846
Now, shall we have Dong Hyeon sing?

1822
01:14:38,016 --> 01:14:39,016
Okay.

1823
01:14:39,086 --> 01:14:40,386
Let's go!

1824
01:14:40,546 --> 01:14:42,315
He brought a nice song.

1825
01:14:42,316 --> 01:14:43,385
A nice song?

1826
01:14:43,386 --> 01:14:45,626
(What does he mean by a nice song?)

1827
01:14:45,986 --> 01:14:47,455
- A nice song. - Here you go.

1828
01:14:47,456 --> 01:14:48,955
"Love is Cruel."

1829
01:14:48,956 --> 01:14:50,355
(The score he got is none other than...)

1830
01:14:50,356 --> 01:14:51,356
"Beethoven?"

1831
01:14:51,357 --> 01:14:53,625
It's the number you sang, Kai.

1832
01:14:53,626 --> 01:14:59,636
(It's the number Kai sang in the musical, "Beethoven.")

1833
01:15:00,966 --> 01:15:02,405
(Feeling a surge of emotion)

1834
01:15:02,406 --> 01:15:04,505
But it's Park Hyo Shin's version.

1835
01:15:04,506 --> 01:15:05,646
Oh, no.

1836
01:15:06,606 --> 01:15:07,975
Well, the score...

1837
01:15:07,976 --> 01:15:09,615
He must've listened to that version more.

1838
01:15:09,616 --> 01:15:10,716
Still!

1839
01:15:11,046 --> 01:15:13,545
He should've used correction tape...

1840
01:15:13,546 --> 01:15:14,986
and written, "Kai's version."

1841
01:15:16,016 --> 01:15:18,056
- Goodness. - I lacked tact.

1842
01:15:18,286 --> 01:15:19,555
You lacked a lot.

1843
01:15:19,556 --> 01:15:20,826
(Embarrassed)

1844
01:15:22,056 --> 01:15:23,195
("Beethoven")

1845
01:15:23,196 --> 01:15:27,226
(He lets go of the embarrassment and starts singing.)

1846
01:15:29,036 --> 01:15:34,536
(He becomes serious when they start singing.)

1847
01:15:35,276 --> 01:15:37,105
(Singing)

1848
01:15:37,106 --> 01:15:38,476
No, that's not right.

1849
01:15:38,646 --> 01:15:40,476
- It was good until this part. - Okay.

1850
01:15:41,206 --> 01:15:42,976
Don't go like this.

1851
01:15:44,116 --> 01:15:46,446
(Singing)

1852
01:15:49,856 --> 01:15:54,026
(The sound projects clearly and powerfully.)

1853
01:15:54,286 --> 01:15:57,096
(Mesmerized)

1854
01:15:57,326 --> 01:15:59,425
Like how you use a claw machine,

1855
01:15:59,426 --> 01:16:01,396
- you need to snatch right away. - Okay.

1856
01:16:01,736 --> 01:16:04,066
You can't go like this.

1857
01:16:05,366 --> 01:16:08,775
(Sing like how you use a claw machine.)

1858
01:16:08,776 --> 01:16:09,836
Like this.

1859
01:16:09,936 --> 01:16:11,905
- But if you don't do that, - Okay.

1860
01:16:11,906 --> 01:16:13,476
then you'll lose directions.

1861
01:16:13,706 --> 01:16:16,145
There are many times when you don't understand...

1862
01:16:16,146 --> 01:16:17,775
professors' directions.

1863
01:16:17,776 --> 01:16:19,215
Yes, there are many times when they say difficult things.

1864
01:16:19,216 --> 01:16:20,646
- You know a burger, right? - Yes.

1865
01:16:21,186 --> 01:16:22,786
You ordered a burger,

1866
01:16:23,156 --> 01:16:25,325
- and there's cheese, lettuce... - That's what I mean by difficult.

1867
01:16:25,326 --> 01:16:26,725
It drives me crazy.

1868
01:16:26,726 --> 01:16:28,026
What burger? What is it?

1869
01:16:28,796 --> 01:16:30,655
(Tell me quickly.)

1870
01:16:30,656 --> 01:16:32,796
- Since there's a sauce inside, - Yes?

1871
01:16:33,326 --> 01:16:35,895
you have to press it hard.

1872
01:16:35,896 --> 01:16:38,935
Imagine that you're taking a small bite.

1873
01:16:38,936 --> 01:16:40,836
(What do you mean, Professor?)

1874
01:16:41,836 --> 01:16:47,206
(Sing like you're pressing a burger.)

1875
01:16:48,076 --> 01:16:49,145
(Confused)

1876
01:16:49,146 --> 01:16:50,316
But how's your version?

1877
01:16:51,746 --> 01:16:53,515
- Maybe you just wanted a burger. - I know.

1878
01:16:53,516 --> 01:16:55,416
- I don't understand what he means. - What is he saying?

1879
01:16:56,056 --> 01:16:58,686
Imagine a gently rippling creek.

1880
01:16:58,956 --> 01:17:01,795
Let's say there's moss floating.

1881
01:17:01,796 --> 01:17:03,055
- Yes. - You use a sieve...

1882
01:17:03,056 --> 01:17:05,296
to remove it.

1883
01:17:05,826 --> 01:17:08,536
You can't scoop up the mud on the bottom of the creek.

1884
01:17:08,836 --> 01:17:11,036
- Okay. I got it. - Don't sing like this.

1885
01:17:11,536 --> 01:17:13,936
Go like this. That way, you can sing more easily.

1886
01:17:14,336 --> 01:17:16,076
- My love - Gosh.

1887
01:17:17,076 --> 01:17:20,246
You have to keep fighting...

1888
01:17:20,446 --> 01:17:22,875
like you're catching a whale with a fishing rod.

1889
01:17:22,876 --> 01:17:24,045
Make it taut.

1890
01:17:24,046 --> 01:17:25,416
Catching a whale with a fishing rod?

1891
01:17:26,016 --> 01:17:27,585
This is what I meant by catching a whale.

1892
01:17:27,586 --> 01:17:29,156
When you hit high notes,

1893
01:17:29,486 --> 01:17:32,226
don't use your strength.

1894
01:17:32,356 --> 01:17:34,986
You need to sing like you're snatching the notes.

1895
01:17:35,196 --> 01:17:37,795
That's what I wanted to say, so I used that expression.

1896
01:17:37,796 --> 01:17:39,595
Talking about it now makes me realise that the students...

1897
01:17:39,596 --> 01:17:40,966
must've not understood clearly.

1898
01:17:41,936 --> 01:17:44,635
Of course, my comparison...

1899
01:17:44,636 --> 01:17:45,936
is a little fairytale-like.

1900
01:17:46,336 --> 01:17:49,076
But that's what singing is like.

1901
01:17:49,476 --> 01:17:51,275
No other theories...

1902
01:17:51,276 --> 01:17:53,075
- can describe it. - That's right. It's hard.

1903
01:17:53,076 --> 01:17:54,975
This is what I always tell my students.

1904
01:17:54,976 --> 01:17:58,245
You should be able to paint with your voice.

1905
01:17:58,246 --> 01:18:01,216
If you want to sing well, you need to have a rich imagination.

1906
01:18:01,616 --> 01:18:03,285
Your voice...

1907
01:18:03,286 --> 01:18:05,425
We often think...

1908
01:18:05,426 --> 01:18:08,556
that our throats burst or that our voices burst out.

1909
01:18:08,626 --> 01:18:10,855
As you hit high notes,

1910
01:18:10,856 --> 01:18:14,265
you have a tendency to make your voice burst out.

1911
01:18:14,266 --> 01:18:16,565
But if you think about it technically,

1912
01:18:16,566 --> 01:18:19,605
the sound closes up as you hit high notes.

1913
01:18:19,606 --> 01:18:22,006
It needs to go through a narrow path.

1914
01:18:22,336 --> 01:18:23,606
Could you give us an example?

1915
01:18:23,906 --> 01:18:25,846
I think I get him.

1916
01:18:26,006 --> 01:18:27,245
You do?

1917
01:18:27,246 --> 01:18:28,845
(You get it?)

1918
01:18:28,846 --> 01:18:31,215
I may not be able to sing, but I get what he means.

1919
01:18:31,216 --> 01:18:32,915
Then get a one-point lesson from him.

1920
01:18:32,916 --> 01:18:34,485
Actually, he's the most eager to sing well.

1921
01:18:34,486 --> 01:18:35,655
- He's the most eager. - That's right.

1922
01:18:35,656 --> 01:18:36,785
- Why are you so eager? - I'm eager too.

1923
01:18:36,786 --> 01:18:38,016
What? Singing?

1924
01:18:38,656 --> 01:18:40,886
(Once he starts singing,)

1925
01:18:41,626 --> 01:18:44,756
(he sings his heart out until everyone gets sick of it.)

1926
01:18:45,296 --> 01:18:49,096
(Music84 loves singing to the point it brings tears to his eyes.)

1927
01:18:49,796 --> 01:18:51,166
Music is for everyone.

1928
01:18:51,536 --> 01:18:53,035
Among the songs with high notes...

1929
01:18:53,036 --> 01:18:54,036
He can sing pop song too, right?

1930
01:18:54,037 --> 01:18:56,305
Shall I try singing high notes with a closed throat?

1931
01:18:56,306 --> 01:18:57,505
- Yes. - I'll sing with my throat closed.

1932
01:18:57,506 --> 01:18:59,276
- Sing while closing it. - You're singing while closing it?

1933
01:19:00,546 --> 01:19:01,975
Gosh, I'm embarrassed all of a sudden.

1934
01:19:01,976 --> 01:19:03,016
Here we go.

1935
01:19:03,216 --> 01:19:04,246
(Warming up)

1936
01:19:04,716 --> 01:19:05,785
(Shaking his head)

1937
01:19:05,786 --> 01:19:07,145
(You're my everything)

1938
01:19:07,146 --> 01:19:08,516
(You're my destiny)

1939
01:19:08,916 --> 01:19:11,456
(I can't break up with you)

1940
01:19:12,456 --> 01:19:14,926
(The higher the notes get,)

1941
01:19:15,426 --> 01:19:17,595
(the more mournful it gets.)

1942
01:19:17,596 --> 01:19:18,626
He closed it well.

1943
01:19:19,096 --> 01:19:21,836
(Somewhere farther away from forever)

1944
01:19:22,496 --> 01:19:25,236
(Tell me you're happy)

1945
01:19:25,936 --> 01:19:26,965
(Heaven)

1946
01:19:26,966 --> 01:19:28,635
He closed it.

1947
01:19:28,636 --> 01:19:30,005
You're applauding?

1948
01:19:30,006 --> 01:19:32,475
- It's because... - His singing contains life.

1949
01:19:32,476 --> 01:19:33,575
- That's right. - Of course.

1950
01:19:33,576 --> 01:19:34,876
I think it's a hard life.

1951
01:19:35,416 --> 01:19:37,875
I took vocal lessons before too.

1952
01:19:37,876 --> 01:19:40,445
- A vocal lesson? - I wanted to hit high notes.

1953
01:19:40,446 --> 01:19:42,355
Actually, hitting high notes...

1954
01:19:42,356 --> 01:19:43,955
No, you can totally hit them.

1955
01:19:43,956 --> 01:19:45,625
- Even he can? - Of course.

1956
01:19:45,626 --> 01:19:47,155
- Really? - Yes, you just...

1957
01:19:47,156 --> 01:19:49,355
Easily put, the start of your voice.

1958
01:19:49,356 --> 01:19:52,495
Imagine the vocal cords aren't here...

1959
01:19:52,496 --> 01:19:54,595
but over here.

1960
01:19:54,596 --> 01:19:56,295
- A head voice. - Yes, just imagine it.

1961
01:19:56,296 --> 01:19:57,365
Imagine that it's here.

1962
01:19:57,366 --> 01:19:58,735
It doesn't go like this.

1963
01:19:58,736 --> 01:20:00,966
The sound comes from here.

1964
01:20:01,466 --> 01:20:02,506
That's it.

1965
01:20:03,176 --> 01:20:04,835
(I'll give it a try too.)

1966
01:20:04,836 --> 01:20:05,846
(Singing)

1967
01:20:07,146 --> 01:20:08,916
(Desperately bringing up his vocal cords)

1968
01:20:09,916 --> 01:20:11,245
Maybe he brought up the voice in the wrong way.

1969
01:20:11,246 --> 01:20:12,285
(Please stop.)

1970
01:20:12,286 --> 01:20:13,545
I think it's much better than before.

1971
01:20:13,546 --> 01:20:14,546
What is?

1972
01:20:15,886 --> 01:20:17,615
- It's because... - You're forcing it.

1973
01:20:17,616 --> 01:20:18,655
What was that?

1974
01:20:18,656 --> 01:20:20,156
I think that was pretty good.

1975
01:20:21,326 --> 01:20:23,725
I explained it to you briefly earlier.

1976
01:20:23,726 --> 01:20:25,696
- You're all seniors. - Yes.

1977
01:20:25,926 --> 01:20:27,665
You should be...

1978
01:20:27,666 --> 01:20:29,965
starting your careers now.

1979
01:20:29,966 --> 01:20:31,136
- Yes. - Then...

1980
01:20:31,266 --> 01:20:34,065
you need to choose good works...

1981
01:20:34,066 --> 01:20:37,405
that fit your characters and voices.

1982
01:20:37,406 --> 01:20:39,005
I think that's the most important part.

1983
01:20:39,006 --> 01:20:40,675
You should consider that too.

1984
01:20:40,676 --> 01:20:41,745
All right.

1985
01:20:41,746 --> 01:20:43,145
Okay, that's all for today.

1986
01:20:43,146 --> 01:20:44,645
- Thank you. - Goodness.

1987
01:20:44,646 --> 01:20:46,445
- Thank you. - "That's all for today."

1988
01:20:46,446 --> 01:20:47,515
Thank you.

1989
01:20:47,516 --> 01:20:48,615
(Thank you, Professor.)

1990
01:20:48,616 --> 01:20:49,816
- I want to learn from him too. - Thank you.

1991
01:20:50,486 --> 01:20:52,825
Prepare for the next class well.

1992
01:20:52,826 --> 01:20:53,826
- All right. - Got it.

1993
01:20:53,827 --> 01:20:55,185
Let's discuss that a lot.

1994
01:20:55,186 --> 01:20:57,256
- All right. - Can I choose a different song?

1995
01:20:58,196 --> 01:20:59,725
The more songs you have, the better.

1996
01:20:59,726 --> 01:21:00,796
All right.

1997
01:21:01,566 --> 01:21:03,535
Can I also choose a different song?

1998
01:21:03,536 --> 01:21:05,566
Yes, choose a different one. Which one do you want to sing?

1999
01:21:06,006 --> 01:21:09,775
Actually, there's a genre I prefer.

2000
01:21:09,776 --> 01:21:11,005
What is it?

2001
01:21:11,006 --> 01:21:12,745
- For example? - Something bright and fun...

2002
01:21:12,746 --> 01:21:14,245
- Hey. - like "Memphis."

2003
01:21:14,246 --> 01:21:15,815
That's good!

2004
01:21:15,816 --> 01:21:18,616
You should be singing a number from "Kinky Boots."

2005
01:21:18,786 --> 01:21:20,245
(Encouraging and supporting sincerely until the end)

2006
01:21:20,246 --> 01:21:21,486
I think you'll be good at it.

2007
01:21:21,646 --> 01:21:23,755
I'll give it a try. Thank you, Professor.

2008
01:21:23,756 --> 01:21:26,055
- Thank you, Professor. - Thank you, Professor.

2009
01:21:26,056 --> 01:21:28,385
The students have stronger faith...

2010
01:21:28,386 --> 01:21:30,525
when they meet a professor who's still active in the field.

2011
01:21:30,526 --> 01:21:32,926
They'd swarm around him to take his advice.

2012
01:21:33,626 --> 01:21:35,535
- Thank you, Professor. - Thank you.

2013
01:21:35,536 --> 01:21:37,136
(Professor Kai, the top in his field, finishes his class.)

2014
01:21:38,766 --> 01:21:41,306
(Kai finally leaves his room.)

2015
01:21:41,866 --> 01:21:43,305
(Sliding)

2016
01:21:43,306 --> 01:21:44,836
(Away)

2017
01:21:45,906 --> 01:21:48,816
(He sets off after changing his status to "away.")

2018
01:21:49,546 --> 01:21:50,616
Is he off work?

2019
01:21:51,376 --> 01:21:53,316
Oh, is it not "away" when he gets off work?

2020
01:21:53,686 --> 01:21:55,355
(He walks past...)

2021
01:21:55,356 --> 01:21:56,815
(the parking lot?)

2022
01:21:56,816 --> 01:21:57,985
He should have dinner now.

2023
01:21:57,986 --> 01:22:00,225
(Is he on his way to have a soulful dinner?)

2024
01:22:00,226 --> 01:22:01,525
- At the cafeteria? - Is he having it at the cafeteria?

2025
01:22:01,526 --> 01:22:02,526
Of course.

2026
01:22:04,656 --> 01:22:05,696
Goodness.

2027
01:22:06,566 --> 01:22:07,566
What...

2028
01:22:08,526 --> 01:22:09,896
- It's a theatre. - It's an auditorium.

2029
01:22:11,366 --> 01:22:14,306
(The students are in the middle of practice.)

2030
01:22:15,676 --> 01:22:18,436
(Amazed)

2031
01:22:19,506 --> 01:22:20,975
(The actors are completely immersed in their roles.)

2032
01:22:20,976 --> 01:22:22,746
So that's how they practice.

2033
01:22:23,516 --> 01:22:26,846
It's a small theatre located within our school.

2034
01:22:27,216 --> 01:22:30,255
We hold a regular performance every semester.

2035
01:22:30,256 --> 01:22:32,885
The students gather almost every day...

2036
01:22:32,886 --> 01:22:34,356
to prepare for it.

2037
01:22:34,726 --> 01:22:37,425
I visited their practice...

2038
01:22:37,426 --> 01:22:40,266
to root for them.

2039
01:22:41,096 --> 01:22:42,936
There's something you gain from such encounters.

2040
01:22:43,136 --> 01:22:45,406
That's why I can't stop appearing in audition shows.

2041
01:22:45,666 --> 01:22:47,265
It feels a little weird.

2042
01:22:47,266 --> 01:22:49,906
After the shoot, I tend to go to the studio.

2043
01:22:49,936 --> 01:22:51,175
- Right away. - So...

2044
01:22:51,176 --> 01:22:52,675
- It's really tiring, - I get what you mean.

2045
01:22:52,676 --> 01:22:54,475
but there are so many things you're inspired by.

2046
01:22:54,476 --> 01:22:55,815
Thanks to the participants?

2047
01:22:55,816 --> 01:22:57,746
After all the classes,

2048
01:22:58,216 --> 01:23:00,686
I can't leave my office easily.

2049
01:23:00,886 --> 01:23:02,216
You're inspired by them.

2050
01:23:03,156 --> 01:23:05,156
(Professor Kai came to get inspired and to root for them.)

2051
01:23:06,786 --> 01:23:08,926
(They exchange greetings quietly so they wouldn't interrupt.)

2052
01:23:10,056 --> 01:23:12,926
(Taking the empty seat)

2053
01:23:13,896 --> 01:23:16,636
(They finally started the musical number confidently.)

2054
01:23:17,336 --> 01:23:20,336
(You look so handsome and sexy)

2055
01:23:22,236 --> 01:23:25,746
(Getting excited as though he's at an actual concert hall)

2056
01:23:26,146 --> 01:23:27,745
It's of high quality.

2057
01:23:27,746 --> 01:23:28,915
They're good.

2058
01:23:28,916 --> 01:23:30,445
The students from my school...

2059
01:23:30,446 --> 01:23:31,716
- They're good. - Yes.

2060
01:23:31,916 --> 01:23:33,685
They also won the grand award...

2061
01:23:33,686 --> 01:23:34,786
from a college musical festival.

2062
01:23:35,086 --> 01:23:37,216
- I bet they did. - They're top-notch.

2063
01:23:39,926 --> 01:23:42,526
(The students show off their top-notch skills they honed.)

2064
01:23:44,226 --> 01:23:46,396
(Doing a sexy body roll)

2065
01:23:46,966 --> 01:23:49,096
(Impressed)

2066
01:23:49,566 --> 01:23:50,866
- They're good. - Yes.

2067
01:23:51,896 --> 01:23:54,335
(Dancing in perfect sync)

2068
01:23:54,336 --> 01:23:56,436
Isn't that the guy from earlier? Is he a different guy?

2069
01:23:56,876 --> 01:23:58,205
The one who asked a question at the end?

2070
01:23:58,206 --> 01:23:59,246
He is.

2071
01:24:02,476 --> 01:24:03,476
(Acting cute)

2072
01:24:03,477 --> 01:24:05,015
I think he'll be good with "Kinky Boots" numbers.

2073
01:24:05,016 --> 01:24:07,215
You're right. He likes lively songs.

2074
01:24:07,216 --> 01:24:08,585
(Proud)

2075
01:24:08,586 --> 01:24:10,386
Through my students,

2076
01:24:10,886 --> 01:24:12,156
I reflect on myself.

2077
01:24:12,456 --> 01:24:14,286
They'd keep me alert.

2078
01:24:14,626 --> 01:24:16,695
There are many things...

2079
01:24:16,696 --> 01:24:18,325
- I learn from them. - You learn from them instead.

2080
01:24:18,326 --> 01:24:19,596
(These days, he learns a lot at the same time.)

2081
01:24:19,896 --> 01:24:21,766
It's true that it's physically tiring,

2082
01:24:22,136 --> 01:24:24,366
but in terms of energy and skills,

2083
01:24:24,766 --> 01:24:26,305
I keep getting refilled.

2084
01:24:26,306 --> 01:24:28,105
- Right, you get refilled. - In that sense,

2085
01:24:28,106 --> 01:24:29,536
it's a school for me as well...

2086
01:24:29,776 --> 01:24:31,636
and a place of learning.

2087
01:24:31,736 --> 01:24:33,546
He must reaffirm his original intention there.

2088
01:24:34,076 --> 01:24:37,545
The desperation from people of that age...

2089
01:24:37,546 --> 01:24:39,315
- That's right. - Their passion is something...

2090
01:24:39,316 --> 01:24:40,715
- you can't learn elsewhere. - That's true.

2091
01:24:40,716 --> 01:24:41,785
- That's the only place. - Right.

2092
01:24:41,786 --> 01:24:42,885
That's right. The desperation.

2093
01:24:42,886 --> 01:24:44,485
- The passion. - The energy from the desperation.

2094
01:24:44,486 --> 01:24:46,285
It's my driving force.

2095
01:24:46,286 --> 01:24:47,586
(The energy from the students is my driving force.)

2096
01:24:47,956 --> 01:24:49,196
Everyone, you are...

2097
01:24:50,756 --> 01:24:51,895
You are really good.

2098
01:24:51,896 --> 01:24:53,465
- Yes! - Thank you!

2099
01:24:53,466 --> 01:24:54,996
- Thank you! - Thank you!

2100
01:24:56,166 --> 01:24:58,365
I can tell how hard you worked...

2101
01:24:58,366 --> 01:25:00,166
from your faces. I love it.

2102
01:25:00,766 --> 01:25:02,006
Here's the thing, though.

2103
01:25:02,436 --> 01:25:05,435
Enjoying it fully onstage...

2104
01:25:05,436 --> 01:25:06,946
is complete faith.

2105
01:25:07,176 --> 01:25:08,816
And I can see it from you guys.

2106
01:25:09,116 --> 01:25:11,945
I hope you'll practice happily just like this. All right?

2107
01:25:11,946 --> 01:25:12,946
- Yes! - Yes!

2108
01:25:12,947 --> 01:25:14,746
- Good luck! - Thank you!

2109
01:25:17,316 --> 01:25:18,655
He's indeed a great professor.

2110
01:25:18,656 --> 01:25:20,256
(A word of encouragement from a senior and a professor)

2111
01:25:22,026 --> 01:25:27,226
(He exits quietly from the students' stage.)

2112
01:25:30,836 --> 01:25:34,965
(Professor Kai's Playbook)

2113
01:25:34,966 --> 01:25:36,436
(Next story)

2114
01:25:36,976 --> 01:25:39,406
(A key player in the Korean band boom, DAY6)

2115
01:25:40,206 --> 01:25:42,316
("DAY6 Writes History, Goyang Stadium Sold Out")

2116
01:25:43,076 --> 01:25:45,376
(The powerful sound of drums that adds to the fun of live performance)

2117
01:25:46,416 --> 01:25:47,615
(The energetic youngest drummer of DAY6)

2118
01:25:47,616 --> 01:25:48,616
Hello.

2119
01:25:49,386 --> 01:25:50,816
(DAY6's Dowoon)

2120
01:25:51,486 --> 01:25:53,556
(It's been 5 to 6 years since he started living alone.)

2121
01:25:54,786 --> 01:25:56,526
(Dowoon's house in all-white)

2122
01:25:57,726 --> 01:25:59,466
(Filled with items that suit him as a musician)

2123
01:26:02,436 --> 01:26:04,766
(Current time is 2:40am.)

2124
01:26:06,236 --> 01:26:08,636
(He busily gets ready since early dawn.)

2125
01:26:09,906 --> 01:26:11,836
(It's been really long.)

2126
01:26:12,776 --> 01:26:14,946
(A pier?)

2127
01:26:15,946 --> 01:26:18,015
(How many webfoot octopuses do you want to catch?)

2128
01:26:18,016 --> 01:26:19,116
(A hundred.)

2129
01:26:20,316 --> 01:26:21,916
(With ambition, the boat sets sail.)

2130
01:26:22,516 --> 01:26:24,156
(My favourite hobby, sea fishing)

2131
01:26:25,216 --> 01:26:26,526
(Shall I show off my skills?)

2132
01:26:27,186 --> 01:26:28,356
(Sturdy)

2133
01:26:28,996 --> 01:26:30,125
Gosh, this one looks good.

2134
01:26:30,126 --> 01:26:31,326
(I'm getting a good feeling.)

2135
01:26:32,426 --> 01:26:33,966
(You're the best, captain.)

2136
01:26:34,696 --> 01:26:36,536
(The person he's visiting to share the webfoot octopus)

2137
01:26:37,066 --> 01:26:38,296
(It's Lee Sung Woo of No Brain, whom he admires.)

2138
01:26:39,206 --> 01:26:40,206
(Why did you catch so many?)

2139
01:26:40,207 --> 01:26:41,406
(You know I'm seasoned.)

2140
01:26:42,376 --> 01:26:44,005
(How much would this cost?).

2141
01:26:44,006 --> 01:26:45,006
(Come on.)

2142
01:26:46,176 --> 01:26:48,246
(Do you want to see Dubu sing?)

2143
01:26:49,276 --> 01:26:51,746
(A Truly Fun Day)

2144
01:26:53,346 --> 01:26:56,116
(After sending off the long-kept stuff...)

2145
01:26:56,556 --> 01:26:57,885
(You guys...)

2146
01:26:57,886 --> 01:26:59,326
(helped me take the first step.)

2147
01:26:59,586 --> 01:27:00,586
(The first step?)

2148
01:27:00,587 --> 01:27:02,086
(Are you saying there will be a second step?)

2149
01:27:02,696 --> 01:27:04,556
(The actual second step for the guys she's grateful to)

2150
01:27:05,296 --> 01:27:07,166
(Pork belly, ramyeon, and makgeolli)

2151
01:27:07,626 --> 01:27:08,965
(I'll grill it deliciously.)

2152
01:27:08,966 --> 01:27:10,266
I mean... Oh, dear.

2153
01:27:10,966 --> 01:27:12,035
(Put it back on. It's okay.)

2154
01:27:12,036 --> 01:27:13,335
(- No. - Don't throw it away.)

2155
01:27:13,336 --> 01:27:15,065
- You could die. - Why would you throw it away?

2156
01:27:15,066 --> 01:27:16,276
(I can laugh, thanks to these guys.)

2157
01:27:17,076 --> 01:27:18,805
(To your grandma and grandpa)

2158
01:27:18,806 --> 01:27:20,006
Good job.

2159
01:27:20,376 --> 01:27:21,376
(Exclaiming)

2160
01:27:22,316 --> 01:27:23,676
(Let's eat in earnest.)

2161
01:27:23,716 --> 01:27:26,586
(Slurping)

2162
01:27:28,746 --> 01:27:30,216
(It tastes amazing after working hard.)

2163
01:27:30,956 --> 01:27:32,586
(It brought back old memories.)

2164
01:27:33,126 --> 01:27:36,596
(Being with them made him feel like he was back in the old days.)

2165
01:27:37,356 --> 01:27:39,596
(I don't know if I can give this to you because it rained.)

2166
01:27:39,726 --> 01:27:41,826
- What is it? - What do you think, Na Rae?

2167
01:27:42,126 --> 01:27:43,936
(Turn around.)

2168
01:27:44,466 --> 01:27:46,666
Take a look. One, two, three.

2169
01:27:48,706 --> 01:27:51,835
(I can't believe it.)

2170
01:27:51,836 --> 01:27:55,716
(Choked up)

2171
01:27:56,346 --> 01:27:58,546
(He choked too before he knew it.)

2172
01:27:59,616 --> 01:28:03,086
(Right now, our hearts are the same.)

2173
01:28:03,786 --> 01:28:05,356
That's our own way.

2174
01:28:05,556 --> 01:28:09,456
(In the Name of Family)
